# HallyuScope _redirects for Cloudflare Pages.
# This file recreates the routing in vercel.json. Rules run top to bottom
# and the first match wins. Status 200 is a rewrite: the visitor keeps the
# clean URL while Cloudflare serves the destination behind the scenes.
# Status 301 is a permanent redirect.
#
# File placement: repo root, which is also the Pages build output directory,
# so the file is deployed as-is. Vercel is untouched: vercel.json still
# controls the Vercel deployment, and the two files must stay in sync.
#
# Note on ordering: static rules come before dynamic rules, as the
# Cloudflare docs ask. The rules below do not overlap, so order changes
# nothing about which rule matches.
#
# What is deliberately NOT in this file:
# - Clean URLs for static pages (/albums, /latest, /review and so on).
#   Pages serves those natively: /albums is served from albums.html with
#   no rule, and /albums.html is 308 redirected to /albums by the platform.
#   A rule like "/albums /albums.html 200" loops: the rewrite serves
#   albums.html, the platform 308s it back to /albums, forever. Verified
#   with wrangler pages dev 2026-11-18: the rule returned a 308 loop.
# - Trailing slashes. Pages also handles those natively: /albums/ is
#   redirected to /albums by the platform, matching vercel.json
#   trailingSlash false. No rule needed.
# - The www to bare domain redirect. _redirects cannot match on host:
#   a source must be a relative path, and Cloudflare lists domain level
#   redirects as unsupported in _redirects. The www redirect is a
#   dashboard Single Redirect instead, documented in
#   docs/cloudflare-deployment.md. The rule below is commented out on
#   purpose: it shows the intent, but it must stay inactive until www DNS
#   points at Cloudflare and the dashboard rule is created.
# https://www.hallyuscope.com/* https://hallyuscope.com/:splat 301

# 1. Sitemap: there is no static sitemap.xml in the repo, the Function builds it.
/sitemap.xml /api/sitemap 200

# 2. Bare dynamic paths rewrite to the Functions too, so legacy
# /article?id=<hex> links keep working.
/article /api/article 200
/album /api/album 200
/concert /api/concert 200

# 3. Dynamic pages: pretty URLs rewrite to the Pages Functions, with the
# slug passed as a query param. These rules are a fallback: the primary
# routing is file-based Functions (functions/album/[slug].js,
# functions/concert/[slug].js, functions/article/[slug].js), which match the
# pretty path directly and do not depend on the rewrite reaching the
# Functions router. Both mechanisms produce the same page.
/article/:slug /api/article?slug=:slug 200
/album/:slug /api/album?slug=:slug 200
/concert/:slug /api/concert?slug=:slug 200
